{"id":"pp-19-bizon","slug":"pp-19-bizon","name":"PP-19 Bizon","aliases":["PP19","PP 19","Bizon-2","Bizon-3","PP Bizon","Bizon SMG","Bizon submachine gun"],"kind":"firearm","category":"submachine-guns","subcategory":"Russian submachine gun","description":"The PP-19 Bizon (Russian: Пистолет Пулемёт Бизон, Pistolet Pulemyot Bizon, Pistol Submachine Gun \"Bison\") is a 9×18mm Makarov submachine gun developed in 1993 by the Russian company Izhmash.","history":"The Bizon was designed by a team of engineers headed by Victor Kalashnikov (son of engineer Mikhail Kalashnikov, creator of the AK-47) and including Alexei Dragunov (youngest son of Yevgeny Dragunov, the creator of the SVD sniper rifle).\nVictor called the Bizon \"The Woman's Gun\" due to it being light with virtually no recoil.\nThe Bizon was developed at the request of the Russian Ministry of Internal Affairs (MVD) and is primarily intended for counter-terrorist and law enforcement units that need accurate fire at close ranges.\nPrototypes were trialed by the Special Equipment Research Institute in 1995 where they outperformed several competitors. The weapon was accepted into service on 28 December 1996.","development":"The Bizon is a lightweight selective fire weapon that fires from a closed bolt, a feature that enhances the gun's practical accuracy.\nThe weapon has a notably large magazine capacity for the size of it, made possible by the uncommon helical design of the magazine.\nIt is based on the AKS-74U and shares 60% of its parts with the AK-74.\nChambered for the standard Russian 9×18mm Makarov pistol cartridge, the Bizon will also fire a new high-impulse armor-piercing 57-N-181SM round.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"9×18mm Makarov"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"9×19mm Parabellum"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":".380 ACP (9×17mm Short)"},{"label":"Cartridge","value":"7.62×25mm Tokarev"},{"label":"Action","value":"Blowback, closed bolt"},{